    Thanks for reply, but that isn't that what I'm talking about  DALLAS SRAM
is used to storage controller configuration. You can easly backup this
configuration to floppy and it isn't a very large problem if you lost it.

    I'm talking about battery module used to keep information stored in cache
memory after power loss. It is some kind of dother board located over cache
memory, flash chips, and mylex chip. If it is not present there is only some
jumper(?!) inserted above cache memory (signed: DAC960LB DB1 MYLEX CORP.)
    Cache memory is Ramtron (Enhanced) EDRAM (looks like normal simm), and if
write back caching method is used there is neccessity to use batery module to
keep data stored in cache after power loss.
    On battery module there is Ni-Cd rechargeable battery (P/N752004 REV.A
2.4V 650mAh E9810C manufactured by ENERGY SALES - meaby that can help). There
is also a red colour LED, that can be easly seen when module is inserted into
controller - I don't know for what (meaby it shows when there is data in
cache?!)
    DEC documentation talks about 24 hours storage time after power-loss (I
suppose that when battery is brand new), and talks nothing about life time of
this battery, and storage time shortage caused by usage and age of battery.
And problem is: Is my data safe in cache memory or not ?!

    Any info will be helpfull.

