Hallo Tom!

 TS> On a DS10, running Tru64 5.1, uplinked to a Cisco Catalyst 3500XL 
 TS> series switch the performance takes a real nose-dive when we try 
 TS> to run 
 TS> at 100half or 100 full.  The switch settings have been set 
 TS> appropriately 
 TS> (no auto negot.).  Setting the DS10 in SRM down to either 10 half 
 TS> or 10 
 TS> full provided optimum performance.  This is really not what we had 
 TS> hoped 
 TS> for.

 TS> Has anyone had success setting the ethernet nics to run at 100 
 TS> half or 100 full?

You must set a) the switch to autonegotiation, and b) use lan_config on 
the Tru64 box to set the NIC to *autonegotiation*, advertising 100MBit 
full duplex. I.e. do not hard lock ANY side of the connection to any 
speed, do not lock anything in SRM at all, but set everything to 
autonegotiate, and only use lan_config in Tru64 to set a hint at which 
speed you wish to be autonegotiated using

lan_config -a 1 -m utp -s 100 -x 1

Expect the autonegotiation to last up to 1 minute (the switch is 
blinking yellow during this time). This is not Tru64 specific, we also 
see this long period of autonegotiation with all other NIC cards and 
operating systems we have tried.

You should also resort to have your Tru64 machines run 24/7, because 
after each reboot, the Tru64 kernel frequently raises some threshold 
parameters of the NIC when you start copying the first few megabytes 
over the net, and each time this threshold is raised, the Catalyst will 
start re-autonegotiating, i.E. each time this happens the network is 
down for appropriately one minute. This infrequent resizing ceases 
after about 2 days of uptime on our site.

We have neither found out how to initially set this threshold to a 
sufficiently high value, nor how to speed up the autonegotiation 
process with the Cisco.

Next time, we'll probably by a switch of a different brand :-X.

Viele Gr��e,
Tobias



