What most likely happened was that a bit cell changed state at some point in
time, and all subsequent reads to that memory location resulted in a single
bit error.  The only way to correct it would be to re-write the location.
Since it appears that you've done a reboot, the location has been rewritten,
and now the problem is gone.  

I'd let it go at that, but keep an eye out in the future for another instance
of this problem at the same memory location.  If you continue to have single
bit errors at this location, I'd replace the associated hardware.  If not,
then don't worry about it.

