When GUI s/w (FrontView) is run on Windows95/98/2000 clients, it takes
a long time (30+ secs) to connect to Tru64 server unless you first add 
an arp entry in the PC client for the Tru64 server.  This time delay
only happens when the client and server are on the same side of the 
router.  When the client has to go thru a router to get to the server,
there is no delay.

Cisco came in to a sister College with the same problem and said that
the problem is slow logins on the Tru64 system.  That can't be the
cause (at least not at our site).  Their answer was to increase the
timeout from the default 10 seconds on the GUI s/w.  My answer is to
add a line to every PC to add a static arp entry for the server.  But
this is tough with Win2000 since adding a static arp entry requires 
Admin priv's.  Ping will work to add a dynamic entry, but it doesn't
last long.

But I don't think either my way or Cisco's is the best answer.  There
has to be some way to make the connection work like it should.  Is
there some setting in Tru64 network setup, or router setup to help this
problem?  I have a minimum network setup on the Tru64.  Just a single
default route to the router gateway.  This is Tru64 4.0g.

Any ideas?

Ray T.


