Ok, here was the full solution.  Some of the help came from
Tony Verhulst and some from Adam Wrzoskowicz.  I was wrong
when I stated that I had single user password protection
enabled.  That confusion on my part comes from dealing 
primarily with Solaris machines where you DO have to enter
the root password to enter Single-User mode.  So whomever
said "Good Lord, what's wrong with just booting to single
user" was absolutely correct.  My apologies for that
misunderstanding.  Now here for posterity in Deja News are
the correct steps:

Cycle the power.  When the blue screen comes up at the
VERY beginning with gibberish like:
0a.0a.2a.3a.5a.   start hitting CONTROL-C like there's
no tomorrow.  Once you get to the   >>>  prompt, type:
>>> boot -fl s

Once it gets into single-user, you should have a # prompt.
Type:
# mount -u /
# mount -a    (basically you want to mount the /usr
              partition here.  If it reports a "dirty
              file system", smack it for being dirty, then
              run "fsck" on the device, e.g.:
              fsck /dev/rdisk/dsk3c    Then do the
              "mount -a" again).
# passwd      Thankfully it doesn't ask for the old 
               password and just lets you give it a new
               password.

Eject the CD-Rom and reboot into multi-user!

Thanks once again to everyone for your help.


